Abjection is the feeling and process of casting something out because it threatens the boundary between “me” and “not me,” order and disorder, human and inhuman.

It’s stronger than ordinary disgust. Disgust says, “I dislike that.” Abjection says:

“That must be expelled because its existence destabilizes who I believe I am.”

Julia Kristeva developed the concept philosophically. Her classic examples include corpses, blood, bodily waste, decay, and exposed wounds—things that were once part of a living person, or reveal that the clean, bounded self is more fragile than it appears.

It can also happen psychologically and socially:

* A person abjects an emotion: “My fear isn’t part of me.”
* A group abjects outsiders to define who belongs.
* Society hides illness, aging, poverty, or death because they rupture its preferred self-image.
* Someone projects their disowned traits onto an enemy and then attacks them there.

In our light-and-shadow language, abjection is trying to eliminate the shadow because recognizing it would alter the self. Integration says, “This exists within or alongside me; I must understand and govern it.” Abjection says, “This cannot be me, so it must be made alien.”

It’s the difference between establishing a healthy boundary and manufacturing a monster to carry what you refuse to recognize.